{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Has HaShem indeed spoken only with Moses.</b> If he separated from his wife on account of the holiness of HaShem, this matter is not forbidden to prophets, for they were not warned concerning this in the Torah. <b>Has indeed only</b> — these are two expressions of restriction, a way of eloquence; and so too (Exodus 14:11) ״המבלי אין קברים״. And the fact that he said 'with Moses' and 'also with us' — because the prophet is a vessel for the holy spirit, therefore the letter bet falls appropriately upon the expression. And this is (Genesis 6:3) ״לא ידון רוחי באדם לעולם״ — it explains that the body of man is a vessel for His spirit, may He be exalted, for the word ידון is from the root of נדנה, as it is written (Daniel 7:15) ״אתכרית רוחי אנה דניאל בגו נדנה״, and its meaning is a vessel." "<b>And HaShem heard.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: they spoke of him even in the presence of Moses, as it is said ״וישמע ה״ — and HaShem heard — and ״האיש משה״ — and the man Moses [is placed adjacent]; and we interpret adjacent occurrences to teach that Moses heard the matter, yet from his surpassing humility he bore it and kept silent, and the Holy One, blessed be He, was zealous for the matter."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The word תיק at the end is translated as 'vessel,' matching the Aramaic נדנה cited from Daniel."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:47.380172615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}